  "details": "Often, when wearing outerwear, it's helpful to be able to delete a portion of the mesh hidden by that outerwear in order to prevent clipping issues. It would be very useful if there was a portable component to do so.\n\nBefore going into how this component should work, let's consider the current options available:\n\n- Splitting meshes: This is heavily discouraged by the VRChat performance ranking system, particularly on Quest. Additionally, if you have multiple, overlapping regions to hide, this results in a combinatorial explosion of meshes.\n\n- Blendshapes to shrink portions of the mesh: This is the most common technique currently in use (\"shrink\" shapes). The downsides of this technique are increased skinning overhead (due to an always-active blendshape), and the inability to actually delete polygons. Because of the latter issue, it's not possible to truly hide a polygon which spans multiple bones, as even if shrunken to zero size it will deform and become visible in some cases.\n\n- UV discard shaders: This can fully delete polygons, but requires specific shader support, is unavailable on Quest, and can't be fully used on renderers with multiple submeshes. In particular, Unity does not allow for animating material properties of just one submesh of a renderer, so the utility of this method is somewhat limited in these cases. These shaders also generally only support a fixed number of areas to discard.\n\n- NaNimation: At least one vertex of each polygon to be deleted is modified, changing one bone to point to a proxy bone (child of the original). When we want to hide the polygon, we animate the bone scale to NaN,NaN,NaN. This is a horrible hack which relies on arguably undefined behavior in Unity (it requires disabling dynamic AABB recomputation, among other things). It requires creating extra bones and (in some cases) constraints. However, it's the most general option currently available, and is being actively used in my Modular Avatar and d4rk's optimizer.\n\n- Infinimation: Attach a blendshape to the mesh, and set its displacement to positive infinity for any vertex we want to disappear. This is also a horrible hack, and again requires disabling dynamic AABB recomputation. It's unsuitable for use in VRChat because it seems to trigger crashes with certain worlds (this was originally reported on my GitHub publicly, so was already disclosed).\n\n- Shadow armatures: For each polygon we want to hide, give it a new set of bones, each parent constrained to the original bone. Then, set the common parent to have scale zero. This however requires a very large number of constraints to implement, particularly if you have multiple overlapping regions to hide, or if you need to deal with non-uniform scaling.\n\nNone of these solutions are fully satisfactory; however, if you were to consider manipulating the index buffer of the mesh dynamically, you can do better. Specifically: If we had a component which could dynamically set the vertex indexes of a set of triangles to 0,0,0, this would hide the polygon cleanly and efficiently. The only dynamic overhead would be at the moment the state is changed (so it would be much more performant on Quest than the other solutions), and it would be compatible with all shaders.\n\nI would suggest an advanced component which is given a list of ranges within the index buffer to hide (or a bitmask), and on enabling, zeroes out those indexes. On disabling, it would restore them. If multiple such components exist, the index is zeroed if it falls within any enabled component. This would provide the primitives we (third-part tool authors) need to build robust and efficient mesh deletion mechanisms.",
